    "Our study has several limitations that should be addressed. First, the results are limited by the retrospective design and the small sample size. Although the only significantly different variable between the ICIs + RT group and the ICIs group was the line of chemoradiotherapy, inherent selection bias remains. Second, the lack of essential biomarker information, such as PD-L1 expression, is a significant limitation. Finally, a notable limitation of this study is its exclusive focus on patients with squamous cell carcinoma, which may limit the generalizability of the findings to adenocarcinoma or other non- squamous cell carcinoma subtypes."
  ]
}

{
  "paraId": "38372E34",
  "style": "",
  "text": "Beyond efficacy, toxicity is another major concern with combination therapy. In a phase 1b trial of RT (60 Gy in 30 fractions) combined with camrelizumab as first-line treatment for locally advanced ESCC, 10 of 19 patients (53%) experienced grade 3-4 adverse events.(28) Toxicity was even more marked in the EC-CRT-001 trial, a phase 2 study of toripalimab combined with definitive chemoradiotherapy (50.4 Gy in 28 fractions) for locally advanced ESCC, in which 36 of 42 patients (86%) experienced grade 3-4 adverse events, including 17 (41%) with nonhematological adverse events, and 1 patient (2%) died of treatment-related pneumonia.(29) Notably, both trials delivered definitive-dose chemoradiotherapy to patients with locally advanced disease, a setting in which the burden of toxicity with triplet therapy appears to be considerable. By contrast, in our cohort of patients with recurrent or metastatic esophageal cancer, grade 3 or higher treatment-related adverse events occurred in only 31% of the ICIs + RT group (Table 3), and no treatment-related deaths occurred. These findings should nonetheless be interpreted with caution, because the retrospective design and small sample size may have led to an underestimation of toxicity; larger prospective studies are needed to define the safety profile of this combination more precisely."
}

